<h1>Prefab files</h1>
<p>A <strong>prefab</strong> is a saved, reusable piece of a game world. It holds a small group of entities — a player character, a door, a pickup — and lets you place that whole group into any scene by name. This page explains what a prefab document contains, how instance IDs are built, and exactly how overrides change prefab contents.</p>

<h2>What a prefab is and why reuse matters</h2>
<p>Imagine you design a player character once: a body sprite, a collider, a script. If every level copies those entities by hand, every fix has to be repeated in every file, and the copies drift apart. A prefab is the single source. Levels place <em>instances</em> of it; editing the prefab updates every placement.</p>
<p>Prefabs also nest: a house prefab can place wall prefabs inside itself. The result is a tree of reusable parts instead of one enormous flat list.</p>
<p>Like every DemiEngine document, a prefab file starts with <code>format_version</code>.</p>

<h2>The document</h2>
<pre data-engine-example="prefab-player"><code>{
  "format_version": 1,
  "id": "prefab://characters/player",
  "entities": [
    {
      "id": "body",
      "components": {
        "Transform2D": { "position": [0, 0] },
        "Sprite": { "texture": "asset://textures/hero" },
        "BoxCollider2D": { "size": [0.8, 1.2] }
      },
      "children": [
        {
          "id": "shadow",
          "components": {
            "Transform2D": { "position": [0, -0.1] }
          }
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}</code></pre>
<p>Field by field:</p>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><code>format_version</code></td><td>Always required. Documents without it are rejected.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>id</code></td><td>The prefab's stable identity as a <code>prefab://</code> URI. <code>prefab://characters/player</code> maps to <code>prefabs/characters/player.prefab.json</code>.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>entities</code></td><td>One list for ordinary entities and prefab placements. Ordinary entries have <code>components</code>; placements have <code>prefab</code> and optional <code>overrides</code>. Use <code>children</code> for local hierarchies.</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>Use <code>entities</code> for entity-prefab authoring, including nested prefab references. A separate top-level <code>instances</code> array is still accepted by the loader, but is not needed or recommended for new documents. UI prefabs have their own <code>*.ui.prefab.json</code> format. Entity fields behave as in <a href="{{ path('docs_page', {slug: 'scene-json'}) }}">Scene files</a>.</p>

<h2>Converting an existing hierarchy</h2>
<p><strong>Scene → Create prefab from selection</strong> creates a source prefab and replaces the selected hierarchy with a linked instance by default. Its existing entity IDs and transforms are retained, so Lua, saved state and other scene references keep using the same IDs. Uncheck <strong>Replace selection with a prefab instance</strong> for a copy without changing the original. Undo restores the original hierarchy and source layout; it leaves the new prefab file available for reuse.</p>
<p>The instance may contain an <code>entity_ids</code> object. Keys are expanded prefab-local IDs (including nested paths); values are stable IDs in the owning scene or prefab document. For example, <code>"entity_ids": {"body": "habitat", "door": "airlock"}</code> keeps those two entity identities instead of producing <code>instance/body</code> and <code>instance/door</code>. Inside another prefab, the outer instance namespace still applies. Override keys remain prefab-local, so <code>body.Transform3D.position</code> still edits the mapped body.</p>
<p>Unmapped entities retain normal prefixed IDs. The mapping uses nonempty string keys and values, every key must resolve to an expanded entity, and destination IDs must be unique across the resulting scene. Missing keys and collisions are validation errors. If a mapped entity is removed from the source prefab, update the instance mapping as part of that authoring change. Copying or duplicating a mapped instance in the editor allocates fresh mapped IDs while preserving the original instance's identities. Instance handles are separate from the runtime entities they own.</p>
<h2>ID expansion: how instance names are built</h2>
<p>By default, when a prefab is placed, its entity IDs are prefixed with the instance ID. This keeps two copies of the same prefab from colliding in one scene.</p>
<p>Worked example. The prefab above has a local entity called <code>body</code>. A scene places it like this:</p>
<pre><code>"entities": [
  {
    "id": "player",
    "prefab": "prefab://characters/player"
  }
]</code></pre>
<p>Expansion rule: <strong>instance ID + "/" + local entity ID</strong>.</p>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Prefab-local ID</th><th>Instance ID</th><th>Resulting scene ID</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><code>body</code></td><td><code>player</code></td><td><code>player/body</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>shadow</code> (child of body)</td><td><code>player</code></td><td><code>player/shadow</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>body</code></td><td><code>enemy_a</code></td><td><code>enemy_a/body</code></td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>So the instance named <code>player</code> turns the local <code>body</code> into <code>player/body</code> in the running scene. Two instances of the same prefab can live side by side — <code>player/body</code> and <code>enemy_a/body</code> — without clashing.</p>

<h2>Nested instances and deeper IDs</h2>
<p>Prefabs can place other prefabs. Suppose <code>prefab://props/wall</code> has a local entity <code>wall</code>, and a house prefab places it:</p>
<pre data-engine-example="prefab-house"><code>{
  "format_version": 1,
  "id": "prefab://props/house",
  "entities": [
    {
      "id": "floor",
      "components": { "Transform2D": { "position": [0, 0] } }
    },
    {
      "id": "wall",
      "prefab": "prefab://props/wall"
    }
  ]
}</code></pre>
<p>Inside the house prefab, that nested instance already expands to <code>wall/wall</code> ("instance <code>wall</code>" + local <code>wall</code>). When a scene then places the house:</p>
<pre><code>"entities": [
  { "id": "house", "prefab": "prefab://props/house" }
]</code></pre>
<p>the prefix chain continues outward:</p>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Source</th><th>ID at that level</th><th>Final scene ID</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>house prefab entity <code>floor</code></td><td><code>floor</code></td><td><code>house/floor</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td>wall prefab entity <code>wall</code> placed as instance <code>wall</code> inside house</td><td><code>wall/wall</code></td><td><code>house/wall/wall</code></td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>Each nesting level adds one prefix segment. Read the final ID from right to left to see which copy you have: <code>house/wall/wall</code> is "the <code>wall</code> entity of the <code>wall</code> placement inside <code>house</code>".</p>

<h2>Overrides</h2>
<p>An <code>overrides</code> object on an instance adjusts the expanded result without editing the prefab. It is written next to the instance's <code>prefab</code> field:</p>
<pre data-engine-example="scene-overrides"><code>"entities": [
  {
    "id": "player",
    "prefab": "prefab://characters/player",
    "overrides": {
      "body": {
        "components": {
          "Sprite": { "color": [1, 0.5, 0.5, 1] }
        }
      },
      "body.Transform2D.position": [3, 2]
    }
  }
]</code></pre>
<p>Keys address prefab content; values describe how much to change. The rules are mechanical:</p>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Case</th><th>What happens</th><th>Example override</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td>Field absent</td>
      <td>Nothing is touched. The expanded entity inherits the prefab's value.</td>
      <td>No <code>rotation</code> key → the prefab's <code>rotation</code> is kept.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Object value</td>
      <td>Nested entity overrides merge recursively: override keys win and other prefab values survive.</td>
      <td><code>"body": {"components": {"Transform2D": {"position": [3, 2]}}}</code> changes position while retaining scale.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Array value</td>
      <td>Replaces the whole array. Elements are not merged one by one.</td>
      <td><code>"body.Transform2D.scale": [2, 2]</code> replaces the full scale pair.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>null</code> value</td>
      <td>Removes that field from the expanded result.</td>
      <td><code>"body.Sprite.texture": null</code> drops the texture field entirely.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Dotted key</td>
      <td>Assigns the value at <code>entity.Component.field</code>. An object assigned this way replaces that field's object rather than recursively merging it.</td>
      <td><code>"body.Transform2D.position": [3, 2]</code> writes only that position.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>The dotted position key writes one field. The nested <code>body.components.Sprite</code> object changes its <code>color</code> while retaining the texture and other source fields. For example, <code>"body.GameplayData.values": {"health": 80}</code> replaces the complete data object. Use that exact-field form when deleted data keys or literal null values must survive composition.</p>
<p>Use overrides for per-placement differences (start position, difficulty tint, starting health). When two placements need the same change, prefer editing the prefab or making a second prefab.</p>

<h2>Children versus <code>parent</code></h2>
<p>Inside a prefab, organize entities with the <code>children</code> array whenever they share the same kind of transform and form one visual unit. The body/shadow example above does this: the shadow follows the body automatically, and no field has to name anyone.</p>
<p>An explicit transform <code>parent</code> field is for spatial links nesting cannot express, such as a relationship across prefab or scene source boundaries. Parent and child transforms must use a compatible spatial domain. Rules of thumb:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Same-domain, visually attached parts → <code>children</code>.</li>
  <li>Cross-prefab or cross-scene spatial relationships → explicit <code>parent</code>, referring to the other entity's stable ID.</li>
</ul>
<p>Keeping ordinary relationships in <code>children</code> keeps documents readable and local: you see the whole unit in one block instead of hunting for IDs.</p>
<p>A child can itself be a prefab placement. Here moving the house root moves both the floor and the expanded wall. IDs stay document-wide: the wall still expands to <code>wall/wall</code>, not <code>house_root/wall/wall</code>.</p>
<pre data-engine-example="prefab-house-children"><code>{
  "format_version": 1,
  "id": "prefab://props/house_grouped",
  "entities": [
    {
      "id": "house_root",
      "components": { "Transform2D": {} },
      "children": [
        { "id": "floor", "components": { "Transform2D": {} } },
        { "id": "wall", "prefab": "prefab://props/wall" }
      ]
    }
  ]
}</code></pre>

<h2>Validation errors</h2>
<p>The loader refuses some shapes because they cannot produce a correct world. Know these limits:</p>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Error</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td>Nested prefab cycles</td>
      <td>If prefab A instances B and B (directly or through more levels) instances A, expansion never ends. Any cycle is an error — the load fails with the chain of prefabs involved.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Mixed domains in one hierarchy</td>
      <td>A <code>children</code> tree or transform parent chain must use compatible spatial domains. An explicit <code>parent</code> does not make a 3D-to-2D transform hierarchy valid.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Hierarchy depth over 128</td>
      <td>The current <code>children</code> traversal rejects depth beyond 128. Prefab-reference cycles are checked separately; this is not a limit on the number of entities or prefab copies.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Unknown prefab URI</td>
      <td>A <code>prefab</code> reference on an entity entry that does not resolve to a prefab file is a validation error.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Duplicate IDs after expansion</td>
      <td>If two expanded entities end up with the same ID, the placement is ambiguous and is rejected.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>Run the validator from the project root to catch all of these before launch:</p>
<pre><code>demi validate games/my_game</code></pre>

<h2>Where prefabs fit</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Scenes place prefabs: <a href="{{ path('docs_page', {slug: 'scene-json'}) }}">Scene files</a>.</li>
  <li>Textures and other referenced files are declared in <a href="{{ path('docs_page', {slug: 'asset-json'}) }}">Asset manifests</a>.</li>
  <li>Behavior attached to prefab entities comes from scripts and the contracts described in <a href="{{ path('docs_page', {slug: 'project-json'}) }}">demi.project.json</a>.</li>
</ul>
